1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,000
A code change does not prove
that a security patch works.

2
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:12,000
I built FixRepro for one repeatable
security regression.

3
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:20,000
This is a controlled synthetic IoT lab
for an over-the-air software update.

4
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:30,000
First, the device starts at version 1.0.0.
The vulnerable gateway checks integrity but ignores signer trust.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:40,000
It accepts an untrusted signed update,
and the device changes to 9.9.0-test.

6
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:43,000
Unsafe behavior reproduced.

7
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:53,000
Next, FixRepro resets and replays the exact same
bytes against the patched gateway.

8
00:00:53,000 --> 00:01:01,000
Digital-signature verification rejects the signer.
The device stays at 1.0.0.

9
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:10,000
Blocking everything would be a bad patch,
so FixRepro runs a positive control.

10
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:19,000
A trusted update succeeds, and the device reaches 1.1.0.
Legitimate behavior is preserved.

11
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:27,000
The matching hashes prove that both tests
used identical input.

12
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:36,000
FixRepro records responses and device transitions,
then creates a tamper-evident evidence bundle.

13
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:45,000
And an independent command checks each file
and recomputes the deterministic verdict.

14
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:49,000
No language model decides it.

15
00:01:49,000 --> 00:01:58,000
From the dashboard, I can load the verified demo
or start a local run.

16
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:03,000
The browser cannot select another target or package.

17
00:02:03,000 --> 00:02:11,000
The same method could fit authorized robotics, smart devices,
industrial IoT, and connected-system release pipelines.

18
00:02:11,000 --> 00:02:15,000
PATCH_VERIFIED applies only to this regression
under these test conditions.

19
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:20,000
FixRepro turns a security fix into a repeatable,
reviewable result instead of an unsupported claim.
